[Line "5.Nf3 Be7 6.Qe2: the Breyer line"]
[Section "3...d5 4.fxe5 Nxe4 5.Nf3"]
[Summary "The classical fifth move as a second weapon. Against the solid 5...Be7 White plays Qe2, takes on c3 with the d-pawn and castles long; the Adams-Anand line is level but full of play."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5! 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. Nf3 { [%cal Gd1e2,Gd2d3] The traditional main move. It
stops ...Qh4+, protects e5 and keeps every option open. It gets full coverage here because the positions
differ in kind from 5.Qf3: the queen goes to e2, the knight is already on its natural square, and there
is no queenless ending.

**Its problem** is theoretical. 5...Bc5 (third line of this section) is the critical reply, and Finkel's
survey in Yearbook 110 argues that it puts 5.Nf3 in doubt. Against everything else White is at least
comfortable. } 5... Be7 { Breyer's quiet move: Black castles first and decides about the knight later. }
6. Qe2 { [%cal Ge2b5,Gd2d3,Ge1c1] 'If in doubt, play Qe2' is Gary Lane's slogan for White in these lines,
and Jonny Hector made the move his speciality. The queen attacks e4 a second time and prepares d3, it
allows dxc3 and queenside castling, and Qb5+ is often available against an undefended b7. It also keeps
the d-pawn at home, so ...Bb4 has no target. }
( 6. d4 { The natural move, building the centre, gives Black two good replies. The first is the old
main line shown here, where taking twice on e4 runs into the pin ...Re8. The second is Goeller's point
6...Bb4!, which reaches the 5...Bc5 6.d4 Bb4 line, where in his view only Black can be better. }
6... O-O 7. Bd3 f5 8. exf6 Bxf6 )
6... Nxc3
( 6... Ng5 { Karpov's way: exchange the f3 knight. } 7. d4 c6 8. Be3 { Better than 8.Qf2 Nxf3+ 9.Qxf3
Qb6!, when Black had counterplay in Hellers-Karpov, Haninge 1990. } 8... O-O 9. O-O-O Na6 10. h4 Nxf3
11. gxf3 { Adams-Xu Jun, Cap d'Agde 1994. The g-file is open and the pawns roll with f4 and h5; White
has the initiative, though Black held the draw. } )
( 6... f5 { Supporting the knight, Bardeleben style. } 7. d3 Nxc3 8. bxc3 { Unclear. This is the one
Qe2 line where the b-pawn takes back, because the d-pawn has already moved; White continues g3, Bg2 and
later d4 or c4. } )
7. dxc3 { [%cal Gc1f4,Ge1c1] The same rule as after 5.Qf3: open the bishop and the d-file, castle
long. }
( 7. bxc3?! { Here the structural recapture is simply slow. } 7... O-O 8. g3 f6 { Black opens the
f-file first and is slightly better (Goeller). } )
7... c5 { Space on the queenside and a square for the knight. Black delays castling on purpose, as the
next note shows. }
( 7... O-O 8. Bf4 c5 9. O-O-O Nc6 10. c4! { The standard break when a rook on d1 faces the queen: the d5
pawn cannot take. } 10... d4 11. Qe4 { Janosevic-Gligoric, Belgrade 1964. The d-pawn is blocked, Bd3
comes with tempo against h7 and White is clearly better. } )
( 7... Nc6 8. Bf4 Be6 9. O-O-O Qd7 10. Qe3 a6 11. Bg5 { [%cal Gf3d4] Exchanging the defender of the dark
squares. } 11... O-O-O 12. Nd4 { Slightly better for White, Janosevic-Reshevsky, Maribor 1967. } )
8. Bf4 Nc6 9. O-O-O Be6 10. h4 { [%cal Gh4h5,Gg2g3,Gf1g2] Gains space and rules out ...g5 against the
bishop; the pawn will go to h5 to fix g7. } 10... h6 11. g3 { The bishop is going to g2 (or h3) to press
on d5 and e6. } 11... Qd7 12. Bg2 O-O-O 13. h5 { Adams-Anand, Linares 1994, drawn after 13...Na5 14.b3
Nc6 15.Qf2 Qc7. Both kings are safe and the centre is fixed. White plays for Nh4 and Bh3 or for the c4
break, Black for ...d4.

**Verdict:** level, with all the pieces on. Goeller suggests 13.Qf1 intending Bh3 as another try. } *

[Line "5.Nf3 Bc5: the critical test"]
[Section "3...d5 4.fxe5 Nxe4 5.Nf3"]
[Summary "The reason theory prefers other fifth moves. 6.Qe2 is the best try: after 6...Bf2+ the king stands well on d1, but 6...Bf5 followed by ...d4 is a real problem."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5! 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. Nf3 Bc5 { [%cal Rc5f2,Re4f2] The move that Tseitlin and
Glazkov, and later Finkel, single out as the challenge to 5.Nf3. Black threatens ...Bf2+ and ...Nf2 and
makes White solve a problem before he has castled. } 6. Qe2 { [%cal Ge2e4] Goeller's choice after working
through the literature. The queen attacks e4, and after a check on f2 the king is not badly placed on
d1. }
( 6. d4 { By far the most common move, and Lane's recommendation. After 6...Bb4 7.Bd2 the bishop has
taken two moves to reach b4, but White's centre is under pressure from ...c5 and ...Bg4. Goeller thinks
only Black can be better; this is the safest line he gives. In current practice Black more often plays
7...c5. } 6... Bb4 7. Bd2 Bg4 8. Be2 Nc6 9. O-O O-O 10. Qe1 Nxd2 11. Qxd2 f6 12. exf6 )
6... Bf2+ { The forcing line, and the one a surprised opponent will choose. }
( 6... Bf5 { [%cal Rd5d4] Mamedyarov's move against Jobava (Warsaw 2013) and the real test: Black just
defends the knight and keeps his threats. } 7. Nd1 { [%cal Gd2d3,Gd1e3] Covers f2 and frees the d-pawn:
d3 will drive the e4 knight away, and the knight on d1 comes back via e3 or f2. } 7... Nc6
( 7... d4! { The critical move according to Finkel. It takes e3 from the knight and c3 from the pawn,
so White cannot untangle normally. } 8. g4 Bg6 9. Bg2 { White's only consistent plan (d3 comes next),
but Finkel's analysis ends in Black's favour. This is the main reason the chapter prefers 5.Qf3. } )
8. d3 Qe7 9. Be3 { Jobava played 9.Ne3 and was gradually outplayed. } 9... O-O-O 10. dxe4 dxe4 11. Nd2 Nd4
12. Bxd4 Bxd4 13. c3 Bxe5 14. Qf2 { Benjamin and Finkel: slightly better for White, who has a piece for
two pawns and is about to consolidate. } )
( 6... Nf2? { Wins the exchange and loses the game. } 7. d4 Bxd4 8. Nxd4 Nxh1 9. g3 c5 10. Nf3 Nc6 11. Bg2
{ The knight in the corner is lost. White will have two pieces for a rook and pawn, with the centre. } )
7. Kd1 { The queen cannot take: the knight defends f2. } 7... Nxc3+ 8. bxc3 { [%cal Gd2d4,Gc1a3] The
exception to the recapture rule. }
( 8. dxc3?! { 8...Bh4! brings the bishop out on the kingside with tempo, and Black is slightly
better. } )
8... Bc5
( 8... Bb6 { Marshall's attempt to save a tempo against d4. } 9. Ba3! { Goeller's improvement on
Torre's 9.d4. The bishop takes the diagonal that Black's has abandoned and Black cannot castle. } )
9. d4 { 9.Qb5+ Nd7 10.d4 c6 11.Qb3 is Velasco's suggestion, about equal. } 9... Be7 10. Qf2
{ [%cal Gf1d3,Gf2g3] The queen clears e2 for the king's bishop and heads for g3. } 10... O-O 11. Bd3 Bg4
( 11... f6 12. Qh4 g6 13. Qh6 fxe5 14. Bxg6 hxg6 15. Qxg6+ Kh8 16. Nxe5 Bf5 17. Qh6+ Kg8 18. Ng6
{ A forced draw. } )
12. h3 Bh5 13. g4 Bg6 14. Bf5 c5 15. h4 Qa5 16. Qe3 cxd4 17. cxd4 Na6 18. Bd2 Qa4 19. Qb3 { Bangiev:
slightly better for White. The king is safe on d1 behind the pawn centre, the kingside pawns have
gained space with tempo and the queens come off on White's terms.

**Verdict:** the 6...Bf2+ line is fine for White. The whole variation stands or falls with 6...Bf5 and
7...d4, and there the published analysis favours Black. } *

[Line "5.Nf3 Bg4 6.Qe2: the Kaufmann line"]
[Section "3...d5 4.fxe5 Nxe4 5.Nf3"]
[Summary "The pin invites two famous traps, Euwe-Yates and Spielmann-Flamberg. If Black avoids them with 6...Nxc3, White castles long with a pleasant game."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5! 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. Nf3 Bg4 { Natural, and a favourite at club level. } 6. Qe2!
{ [%cal Ge2b5,Gc3e4] Arthur Kaufmann's move. Black has to decide about the e4 knight at once, and with the
bishop gone from c8 the check on b5 picks up b7 in several lines. }
6... Nxc3 { The only move that keeps the balance. }
( 6... Nc6? { The common error: it develops and seems to defend everything. } 7. Nxe4 Nd4
( 7... dxe4 8. Qxe4 Qd7 9. Bb5 { White is a clean pawn up. } )
8. Qd3 Bxf3 9. Ng3! { The quiet retreat that is easy to miss: Euwe-Yates, The Hague 1921. The bishop on
f3 and the knight on d4 are both attacked and Black loses a piece. In the 1925 theme tournament Smirka
played 9.gxf3 and Torre escaped. } )
( 6... Nc5?! { Keeps the knight but loses time. } 7. d4 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Qh4+ 9. g3 Qxd4 10. Be3 Qxe5
11. O-O-O { Two pawns down, with every piece out and the black queen in the firing line. } 11... c6
12. Nxd5! { The d-file is opened by force. } 12... cxd5 13. Rxd5 Qe6 14. Bc4 Qe4 15. Bxc5
{ Spielmann-Flamberg, Mannheim 1914: 15...Qxf3 16.Re1+ and mate follows. Black should prefer 8...Ne6
9.Be3, when White is only much better. } )
( 6... Ng5 7. d4 { Goeller thinks 7.h4 may be stronger still. } 7... Nxf3+ 8. gxf3 Qh4+ 9. Qf2 Qxf2+
10. Kxf2 Be6 11. Be3 { Barle-Pavasovic, Ljubljana 1994: a slightly better ending thanks to the centre
and the open g-file. } )
7. dxc3 { 7.bxc3 is playable too (Tholfsen-Torre 1925), but the d-pawn is consistent: Bf4 and O-O-O. }
7... c6
( 7... Nc6 8. Bf4 Qd7 9. O-O-O O-O-O 10. Qe3 { With h3 and Bb5 to come. Mallahi-Quintero, Yerevan 1999,
was clearly better for White once the pin was broken and c4 was played. } )
8. Bf4 Qa5 { Discouraging O-O-O by looking at a2. } 9. h3 { Asks the bishop where it is going before
White commits his king. } 9... Qa4?! { Norwood's move in the model game. It hits f4 and stops c4, but it
costs a tempo and White simply improves the bishop. } 10. Bg5 { [%cal Ge5e6] Torre's idea. The bishop
keeps Black's king in the centre (no ...O-O-O, no ...Be7) and supports e5-e6. } 10... Be6 { The most
solid reply according to Goeller. } 11. Nd4 Bc5 12. Nxe6 fxe6 13. b4 Bb6 14. Qh5+ g6 15. Qg4 Kd7 16. Bd3
{ Slightly better for White (Goeller): two bishops, and a black king that has to live on d7. } *

[Line "5.Nf3 Nc6 6.Bb5, and 5...Bb4"]
[Section "3...d5 4.fxe5 Nxe4 5.Nf3"]
[Summary "Against the knight move White pins first and then plays Qe2; the knight is chased to e6 and White keeps a small plus. 5...Bb4 6.Qe2 can end in a queen sacrifice."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. f4 d5! 4. fxe5 Nxe4 5. Nf3 Nc6 { Development with an eye on e5. }
( 5... Bb4 { Pinning the knight that attacks e4. } 6. Qe2! { Once more. After the exchange on c3 the
d-pawn recaptures and the bishop pair is White's. } 6... Bxc3 7. dxc3 O-O 8. Bf4 f6? { The thematic
break fails tactically here. }
( 8... c6 9. O-O-O Qa5 10. Kb1 { Slightly better for White (Goeller): after 10...Qb6 11.Qe1 the knight
on e4 is short of squares. } )
9. O-O-O fxe5 10. Qxe4! { A queen sacrifice that Spielmann missed against Cohn in 1908 (he took on e5
and won anyway). } 10... dxe4 11. Bc4+ Kh8 12. Rxd8 Rxd8 13. Nxe5 { Nf7+ is threatened and Black cannot
meet it properly; Goeller gives the position as winning. } )
6. Bb5 { [%cal Gb5c6] The pin comes first: it secures e5 and makes ...Bc5 less effective. The immediate
6.Qe2 Bf5 gave White nothing in Berg-Spassky, Bundesliga 1987. } 6... Be7
( 6... Bc5 { The most common move in practice. } 7. d4 Bb6
( 7... Bb4 8. O-O { White has castled and ...Bxc3 only strengthens his centre. } )
8. O-O O-O 9. Na4 { Removing the bishop that presses on d4. } )
7. Qe2 Nc5
( 7... Nxc3 8. dxc3 O-O 9. Bf4 { The familiar set-up. The break 9...f6 fails to 10.O-O-O fxe5
11.Rxd5!. } )
( 7... Ng5 8. d4 Nxf3+ 9. Qxf3 O-O 10. Be3 { Turner-Motwani, British Championship 1994. White has the
centre and castles long. } )
8. d4 Ne6 9. Be3 O-O 10. O-O
( 10. h4 { Bangiev's sharper recommendation: 10...h6 11.O-O-O Nb4 12.Qd2 c6 13.Be2 Re8 14.g4 with the
initiative. The choice when a win is needed. } )
10... a6 11. Ba4 Na5 12. Qf2 Nc4 13. Bb3 Nxe3 14. Qxe3 c6 15. Ne2 { Yegiazarian-Estrada Nieto, Linares
1999: slightly better for White. Black has the bishops but no pawn break, while White can play c4
against d5 and use the f-file. } *
